task: dataflow-onepass
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-onepass
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfo

spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 3

why: the engine reads Wrocław's feed THREE TIMES where §20's
  hand-written lane reads it once, because stage 1 allows one keyed
  stage per flow and the job has three. Until that is fixed no number
  the engine produces is comparable with that table, and both
  previous lanes said so rather than quoting one.

  It is also where docs/benchmarks.md §20 locates the real asymmetry
  between okay and an engine: "a fan-out in one JVM is calling three
  methods with the same reference. In Flink the same fan-out is three
  shuffles."

what: `Sink[A, R]` — a keyed or windowed stage together with what its
  output is folded into — and `and`, which pairs two of them into a
  `Sink[A, (R1, R2)]`. One pass over the source feeds all of them.
  The shape is deliberately `Aggregator.zip`'s: the core has computed
  two statistics in one pass since P1, and this is the same idea one
  level up.

  Seeding survives the fan: a Sink declares the event-time functions
  its windows need seeded, `and` concatenates them, and the driver's
  one pre-pass computes a prefix maximum per column.

scope, stated so it is not discovered later: a fan finishes by MERGE.
  The exchange stays available to single-stage flows. That is not
  laziness — stage 2 measured the crossover at ~100 000 accumulators
  and a fan of Wrocław-sized stages is three orders under it, so
  per-sink exchange inside a fan is machinery nothing has asked for.
  If something asks, it goes in the backlog with this sentence.

predict: the three-flow Wrocław run should collapse to roughly a
  third of its passes and NOT to a third of its time — the windowing
  and the aggregation dominate, not the source. Measure it rather
  than assert it.
